.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-RESTAMP-IDENTITY 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-restamp-identity
.Nd take another machine's identity out of a boot environment before it is booted here
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -dataset Ar dataset | Fl -root Ar dir
.Op Fl -hostname Ar name
.Op Fl -new-hostid
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A boot environment carries all of
.Pa /etc .
Replicate one to twenty machines and all twenty wake with one machine id,
one set of SSH host keys, and network connections pinned to the first
machine's network card.
Nothing fails at once: they boot, then the journal and D-Bus confuse them,
every SSH client that trusted one trusts them all, and the pinned
connections never come up.
.Pp
.Nm
clears, in the boot environment given:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/machine-id
emptied; systemd makes a new one at first boot;
.It Pa /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
removed;
.It Pa /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*key*
removed; made again at first boot;
.It the journal, Pa wtmp No and Pa btmp
the old machine's history;
.It Ql mac-address= No in NetworkManager connections
removed.
A connection pinned to an interface name is reported, not changed: on a
machine with several cards, renaming it blind could put it on the wrong
one.
.El
.Pp
It keeps
.Pa /etc/hostid
unless told otherwise: the boot environment is going to a machine that
already owns its pool, and ZFS checks the host id at every import.
.Pp
Then it checks that each of those is so, and records when and where it
ran in
.Pa /etc/kldload/restamp.info .
.Pp
It refuses the running boot environment, and
.Pa / :
clearing the identity of the system in use is noticed only at its next
boot, remotely.
It needs root.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -dataset Ar dataset
A boot environment, mounted for the purpose under
.Pa /tmp
and unmounted after.
.It Fl -root Ar dir
A boot environment already mounted at
.Ar dir .
.It Fl -hostname Ar name
Write
.Ar name
to its
.Pa /etc/hostname .
.It Fl -new-hostid
Remove
.Pa /etc/hostid
as well, for a boot environment going to different hardware.
On the same machine this makes every pool import warn that the pool was
last used by another system.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Restamped and checked.
.It 1
A step or a check failed.
.It 2
A usage error, or refused.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30, against a throwaway dataset
.Ql rpool/rstest-3937594
filled like a boot environment \(em a machine id, a D-Bus id, an SSH host
key, a host id, a journal, and a connection pinned to both a MAC address
and an interface \(em and destroyed afterwards.
.Ss Restamping
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-restamp-identity --dataset rpool/rstest-3937594 --hostname node7
mounted rpool/rstest-3937594 at /tmp/restamp.L3OiM4
restamping identity in /tmp/restamp.L3OiM4
  cleared /etc/machine-id (systemd-machine-id-setup regenerates it)
  removed /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
  removed SSH host keys (sshd-keygen regenerates them)
  KEPT /etc/hostid: this machine owns its pool, and ZFS checks it
  cleared journal and login records
  WARNING: br0-port.nmconnection pins interface-name=enp5s0 — check it matches a NIC here
  removed 1 MAC pin(s) from NetworkManager connections
  hostname set to node7
verifying:
  ok    machine-id is empty
  ok    no SSH host keys remain
  ok    no dbus machine-id
  ok    hostid preserved
  ok    hostname is node7
OK: identity restamped. First boot regenerates machine-id and SSH host keys.
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-restamp-identity --dataset rpool/ROOT/onyx
FATAL: rpool/ROOT/onyx is the RUNNING boot environment — refusing
# (exited 2)
# kldload-restamp-identity --root /
FATAL: --root / is the running system — refusing
# (exited 2)
# kldload-restamp-identity --dataset rpool/rstest-3937594 --hostname "bad'name"
FATAL: not a valid hostname: bad'name
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssh-keygen 1 ,
.Xr machine-id 5 ,
.Xr kldload-restore-machine 8 ,
.Xr kldload-seal 8 ,
.Xr zfs-send 8
.Sh HISTORY
It is the boot-environment form of what kldload already did for cloned
virtual machines, which each get their own machine id.
Unlike
.Xr kldload-seal 8 ,
which prepares an image for hardware not yet known and so removes the host
id, it keeps it by default.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: a host name was written unchecked and
pasted into its own check, and every run left its mount point in
.Pa /tmp .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
The running boot environment is recognised by its name; a copy of it under
another name is not the running one, and is restamped.
.Pp
Keys other than the SSH host keys \(em WireGuard's, a TLS key, the
machine's certificate authority \(em are not touched, and two machines
booted from one environment share them.
